WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.335
-Welcome back.
I'm talking with Common.

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:03.503
I always love
talking to you, buddy.

00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:05.838
The new Audible original
is available tomorrow.

00:00:05.838 --> 00:00:07.707
It's called "Bluebird Memories:

00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:11.143
A Journey
Through Lyrics and Life."

00:00:11.143 --> 00:00:12.745
Tell me about the concept,

00:00:12.745 --> 00:00:16.082
and how would you describe this
to listeners out there?

00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:20.353
-Well, I would describe it
as a combination

00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:24.089
of theater and live performance.

00:00:24.089 --> 00:00:26.659
Because we did it at a theater,
we performed it at a theater,

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:28.527
but it's a written piece.

00:00:28.527 --> 00:00:30.229
It's kind of
a new expression from me,

00:00:30.229 --> 00:00:33.666
because I wanted to combine
what I do as an actor,

00:00:33.666 --> 00:00:36.168
and I've been yearning
to do live theater,

00:00:36.168 --> 00:00:39.805
and what I've done as a musician
and a live performer.

00:00:39.805 --> 00:00:43.108
So, I came up with this concept
where I wanted

00:00:43.108 --> 00:00:46.879
to write a letter to my father,
who passed in 2014,

00:00:46.879 --> 00:00:50.016
and the letter would be
a journey through my life

00:00:50.016 --> 00:00:52.819
and through my career
and through the music,

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:55.622
but also just who I am
as a human being.

00:00:55.622 --> 00:00:59.225
And it would show the laughter,
the good times,

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:02.895
the times I missed him, the
times I might have been angry,

00:01:02.895 --> 00:01:06.199
but also just the times we
celebrated and had a great time.

00:01:06.199 --> 00:01:08.667
And I really think it's
the journey of a human being,

00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:11.437
a man going through life,

00:01:11.437 --> 00:01:14.573
and it's told
through these monologues

00:01:14.573 --> 00:01:15.975
and through the music.

00:01:15.975 --> 00:01:18.444
-Yeah, I loved
in the Audible original,

00:01:18.444 --> 00:01:22.047
you talk about your dad buying
your first rap album.

00:01:22.047 --> 00:01:23.716
-Yeah, yeah.
That was --

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:26.619
Yeah, so my dad,
he come to town, and he took me

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:29.355
to a record store that we had
in my neighborhood,

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:30.990
called Metro Music.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:34.093
That's where I would get
some of my music, you know?

00:01:34.093 --> 00:01:35.929
And he said, "What do you
want me to get you?"

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:39.832
and I said, "Yeah, can you get
me this rap song right here?

00:01:39.832 --> 00:01:42.501
It's called 'The Message,'
by Melle Mel,

00:01:42.501 --> 00:01:44.904
Grandmaster Flash,
and the Furious Five."

00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:47.640
And he was like, "What?"
You know, like --

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.809
'Cause rap wasn't his thing.

00:01:49.809 --> 00:01:51.377
He wasn't really too much
up on rap.

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:56.181
He was a doo-wop, a jazz dude.
So he was like, "What?"

00:01:56.181 --> 00:02:01.421
And then, you know, hey,
he bought it, he got it for me.

00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:04.156
I put it on,
the story goes, man.

00:02:04.156 --> 00:02:06.559
I started writing.
Like, maybe two weeks later,

00:02:06.559 --> 00:02:09.362
I wrote my first rap, you know?
-That's awesome.

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:12.231
I'm trying to think of my --

00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:14.533
the records I would make
my dad get me.

00:02:14.533 --> 00:02:17.203
We were into 45s at the time.

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:19.138
-What did you like?
What did you get?

00:02:19.138 --> 00:02:21.974
What was one of...?
-I wanted Musical Youth.

00:02:21.974 --> 00:02:24.076
"Pass the Dutchie" was my jam.
-Oh, yeah.

00:02:24.076 --> 00:02:26.812
-Ooh! Ooh!
I used to love that!

00:02:26.812 --> 00:02:28.947
-And he came home, he's like --
Dad's got two 45s,

00:02:28.947 --> 00:02:32.017
and me and my sister
are like, "What?! Oh, my gosh!"

00:02:32.017 --> 00:02:36.088
And it was "Mandy,"
by Barry Manilow,

00:02:36.088 --> 00:02:39.292
and some medley
of The Beach Boys.

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:42.695
And we were like, "Okay."
-[ Laughing ]

00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:45.331
Like, again, now I'm older,

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:47.199
and I'm like,
"Oh, they were great jams,"

00:02:47.199 --> 00:02:49.635
but I was like,
"Anything on the top 40

00:02:49.635 --> 00:02:52.871
that we were pointing out,
we didn't get that at all."

00:02:52.871 --> 00:02:55.073
Eventually, I ended up getting
the Musical Youth one,

00:02:55.073 --> 00:02:58.544
but I remember it was
Barry Manilow and Beach Boys,

00:02:58.544 --> 00:03:00.179
and then I just started going --

00:03:00.179 --> 00:03:02.214
I started flipping
through his 45s

00:03:02.214 --> 00:03:04.817
and just playing doo-wop,
like you were saying,

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:07.453
or when you get
to hear the drums

00:03:07.453 --> 00:03:09.055
on The Rolling Stones
or something like that.

00:03:09.055 --> 00:03:13.359
I remember him playing
"Honky Tonk Woman,"

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:14.694
Rolling Stones, and he was like,

00:03:14.694 --> 00:03:18.297
"Just watch the woofer
when you put this on,"

00:03:18.297 --> 00:03:22.168
and it was like, "Ba bum, ck,
buh-ck, boo boo-ck,"

00:03:22.168 --> 00:03:23.836
and you see the thing moving.

00:03:23.836 --> 00:03:25.671
I was like, "Whoa,
the speaker's moving.

00:03:25.671 --> 00:03:27.006
I didn't even know
it was that --"

00:03:27.006 --> 00:03:28.774
But I loved any of that stuff.

00:03:28.774 --> 00:03:30.543
-I loved getting introduced
to those things,

00:03:30.543 --> 00:03:32.878
but, you know, when you a kid,
you want to hear your music.

00:03:32.878 --> 00:03:35.982
Like, I roll around
with my daughter,

00:03:35.982 --> 00:03:38.050
I would take her ice-skating
with her friends,

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:40.653
and I remember playing
Lauryn Hill.

00:03:40.653 --> 00:03:42.287
She claims
I was playing Michael Jackson,

00:03:42.287 --> 00:03:44.990
but I say I was playing
Lauryn Hill, and she said --

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:47.327
she was like,
"Dad, can you put on music

00:03:47.327 --> 00:03:50.663
that was made after '98?"
and I was like...

00:03:50.663 --> 00:03:53.732
-[ Laughing ]

00:03:53.732 --> 00:03:56.569
It happens to generations
and generations.

00:03:56.569 --> 00:03:58.504
It's so fun, right?

00:03:58.504 --> 00:04:00.172
-Yeah, I'm just like,
"Yo, chill out.

00:04:00.172 --> 00:04:02.875
Like, this is classic."
Now she loves Lauryn Hill.

00:04:02.875 --> 00:04:04.944
Now she loves Lauryn Hill.
-Yeah, of course.

00:04:04.944 --> 00:04:07.413
And I know every doo-wop song
in the world.

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:10.282
Yeah, it's like --
It all --

00:04:10.282 --> 00:04:12.952
-Generations.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:04:12.952 --> 00:04:14.286
Great catching up
with you, Common.

00:04:14.286 --> 00:04:15.487
I love talking to you.

00:04:15.487 --> 00:04:17.089
Congrats on
the Audible original.

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:19.458
It's out tomorrow --
"Bluebird Memories:

00:04:19.458 --> 00:04:21.727
A Journey
Through Lyrics and Life."

00:04:21.727 --> 00:04:24.863
Please come back. I want
to see you in person, bud.

00:04:24.863 --> 00:04:27.233
-Yes, I will see you
in person, man. God bless.

00:04:27.233 --> 00:04:29.235
Be well, Jimmy, a'ight?
-You too. Bye, bud.

